WARN: No HF token. HF-backed tools (hf_to_uc, papers, github docs) will refuse to run. Set HF_TOKEN if you need them.
Model: databricks/databricks-claude-sonnet-5
Max iterations: 150
Prompt: You are the ML intern on Databricks. Build a Kaggle Playground S6E8 ("Predicting
Smartphone Addiction") submission end-to-end.
DATA (already staged in a UC Volume)
- Train: /Volumes/serverless_lakebase_praneeth_catalog/ml_intern_test/scratch/s6e8/train.csv   (691,369 rows)
- Test:  /Volumes/serverless_lakebase_praneeth_catalog/ml_intern_test/scratch/s6e8/test.csv    (296,302 rows)
- Sample submission: .../s6e8/sample_submission.csv
- Columns: id, age, daily_screen_time_hours, social_media_hours, gaming_hours,
  work_study_hours, sleep_hours, notifications_per_day, app_opens_per_day,
  weekend_screen_time, gender, stress_level, academic_work_impact, addicted_label
- TARGET (train only): addicted_label (binary 0/1), 70.94% positive
- Competition metric: ROC-AUC. Submission needs a probability, not a hard label.
BEFORE YOU WRITE MODEL CODE
1. read_skill("kaggle-tabular-classification"). It carries measured results from this
   exact competition, including a table of ideas already falsified — do not spend
   compute re-deriving those.
2. Call the compute_advice tool (task_shape="tabular", model_family="xgboost",
   n_rows=691369, n_features=12) and follow what it says. This is tabular gradient
   boosting, so the answer is CPU: use kind="serverless". Do not use serverless_gpu
   here — LightGBM cannot even initialise on GPU on that image (no OpenCL).
3. Confirm the target from sample_submission.csv rather than assuming it.
TASK
1. Inspect schema, class balance, and per-column missingness (4-19% per column here).
2. Train with StratifiedKFold(5, shuffle=True, random_state=42) on original row order.
   There is no temporal or group column, and id carries no signal — do not invent a
   cleverer split, and do not reuse the s6e5 time-holdout lesson, which does not apply.
3. Feature work, in this order (the skill explains why each, with numbers):
   - target encoding (nested inside folds, smoothing=10) + transductive frequency
     encoding, over ALL 12 columns cast to string levels including numerics
   - imputed columns kept ALONGSIDE the NaN-bearing originals, never replacing them
   - composition/ratio features, including the accounting residual
     daily_screen_time_hours - (social_media_hours + gaming_hours + work_study_hours)
   - do NOT add NaN-indicator features: missingness is MCAR and they measure -0.00001
4. Train XGBoost, LightGBM, and CatBoost; then blend in LOGIT space with a
   LogisticRegression meta-learner, fitted so no row is scored by a combiner that saw
   its own OOF value. Keep predictions in float64.
5. Log params, per-fold AUC, OOF AUC, and feature importances to MLflow. Register the
   best model to UC. Call infer_signature() and pass input_example= on log_model —
   registration fails without a signature.
6. Write submission.csv (columns: id, addicted_label) back to the Volume. Verify it has
   exactly 296,302 rows and that its id column matches sample_submission.csv in order
   BEFORE reporting done.
7. Final report: per-fold and OOF AUC per model, the blend weights, top features, UC
   model URI + version, MLflow run id, first 5 submission rows, and any failure modes
   you hit with how you worked around them.
CONSTRAINTS
- Headless. CPU compute. Budget: 45 min wall clock.
- WRITE THE TRAINING SCRIPT INCREMENTALLY. Do not compose the whole file inside one
  reply — a long script will exhaust your output-token budget before you emit any tool
  call, and then nothing runs. Build it in 3-4 chunks: `bash` with
  `cat > /tmp/s6e8_train.py <<'PY' ... PY` for the first chunk, then append the rest
  with `cat >> ... <<'PY'`. Verify with `python -c "import ast;ast.parse(open(...).read())"`,
  then submit the job with `script_path="/tmp/s6e8_train.py"` — databricks_jobs reads the
  file and stages it for you. Do NOT paste the script back into the `script` argument,
  and never submit a placeholder.
- Do NOT modify agent source code — workarounds go inside the job script.
- Report OOF honestly. A number you did not measure is worse than no number.
CALIBRATION (so you know when to stop)
- A hand-tuned LGBM with no feature work scores ~0.9632. That is the naive attempt.
- A competent single model reaches ~0.9684 OOF, which maps to ~0.9698 public LB.
- A 3-4 model logit stack reaches ~0.9697 OOF, roughly 0.9710 LB.
- Public LB top is 0.97142, but above ~0.97110 the evidence says people are blending
  each other's public submissions and overfitting a 59k-row split. Do not chase it.
- The CV->LB offset on this competition is positive, +0.0011 to +0.0015: LB reads
  slightly higher than OOF because test predictions average five fold-models.
WORKSPACE BINDINGS FOR THIS RUN
- UC model target: serverless_lakebase_praneeth_catalog.ml_intern_test.s6e8_addiction_clf
- MLflow experiment: /Shared/databricks-ai-intern/s6e8 (re-root on directory collision)
- Write submission to: /Volumes/serverless_lakebase_praneeth_catalog/ml_intern_test/scratch/s6e8/submission_agent.csv
- Stage local scratch files under /tmp, NOT under the repo working directory.
